   207	
   208	### Harness: Codex CLI (`--harness codex`)
   209	
   210	The framework runs its **same** enforcement hooks under OpenAI Codex CLI.
   211	`--harness codex` does not fork any hook — it emits a Codex-side
   212	registration that invokes the shared Python hooks with
   213	`CEO_HOOK_ADAPTER=codex`. **Verified against codex-cli 0.139.0** (pin
   214	`.claude/governance/codex-cli-pin.txt` = `>=0.128.0,<0.140.0`).
   215	
   216	```bash
   217	./scripts/install.sh /path/to/your-app --harness codex
   218	```
   219	
   220	**What the codex path emits (in addition to the base install):**
   221	
   222	- `.codex/hooks.json` — registers PreToolUse (`apply_patch|Edit|Write`,
   223	  `^Bash$`, `mcp__.*`), PostToolUse `*`, SessionStart, UserPromptSubmit,
   224	  Stop, SubagentStart, and a turn-ended backstop, each invoking the shared
   225	  `_python-hook.sh`-shimmed hooks with `CEO_HOOK_ADAPTER=codex` and a
   226	  per-hook timeout.
   227	- `.codex/rules/ceo.rules` — a COARSE `prefix_rule(decision=forbidden)`
   228	  execpolicy backstop for the destructive-command classes (never coverage
   229	  — the hook owns the real parser).
   230	- operator `AGENTS.md` — the operator contract, rendered ≤ 32768 bytes
   231	  (`project_doc_max_bytes`).
   232	- a manifest ledger `.codex/.ceo-harness-manifest` so `uninstall.sh`
   233	  removes every emitted path (lifecycle symmetry).
   234	
   235	**Enforcement is per-rail — do not read "runs on Codex" as "same
   236	guarantees as Claude Code".** canonical-edit, bash-safety, plan-lifecycle,
   237	kernel-deny, config, and kill-switch are ENFORCED at edit time; the audit
   238	HMAC chain is ENFORCED but completeness-bounded (partial shell interception
   239	means absence of an entry is not evidence of absence of activity); the
   240	pair-rail is inverted and PARTIAL (Codex operates, `claude -p` reviews at
   241	Stop-time and push-time); **spawn governance is ADVISORY, not enforced**
   242	(`continue:false` is parsed but does not stop the subagent on 0.139). The
   243	full per-rail matrix with residuals is
   244	[`docs/provider_capability_matrix.md`](docs/provider_capability_matrix.md).
   245	
   246	#### Trust flow (consent-first) — nothing is enforced until you grant it
   247	
   248	Codex requires two separate trust grants before a project hook fires, and
   249	**the installer never writes trust for you** — it prints what to trust and
   250	asks you to confirm:
   251	
   252	1. **Project trust** — `projects."<path>".trust_level = "trusted"` in your
   253	   Codex user config.
   254	2. **Per-hook trust** — the `/hooks` review flow (or the headless
   255	   `[hooks.state]` entries), keyed to the hook **registration** hash.
   256	
   257	An installed-but-untrusted hook is a **silent no-op**, indistinguishable
   258	from healthy at runtime. The installer's final line is a post-install
   259	arming check that reports **ARMED / NOT-ARMED-(untrusted) / BROKEN** — read
   260	it. `BROKEN` also flags the two known 0.139 substrate gaps: hook discovery
   261	returns zero hooks inside a **git worktree** (use a plain clone), and a
   262	Codex version outside the pin range.
   263	
   264	#### Trust-rekeying friction (know this before upgrading)
   265	
   266	Codex `/hooks` trust is keyed to the hook **registration** (the command
   267	string), **not** the hook program body:
   268	
   269	- A framework upgrade that changes only hook `.py` bodies does **not**
   270	  re-prompt for trust — low friction (the "every upgrade re-prompts" fear
